WEBVTT

1
00:00:15.012 --> 00:00:17.254
Mary looked at the key for quite a long time.

2
00:00:18.435 --> 00:00:20.256
She turned it over in her hand, thinking.

3
00:00:21.357 --> 00:00:25.720
If this were the key to the secret garden, all she had to do now was find the

4
00:00:25.780 --> 00:00:30.964
door! She liked the idea of going into the garden and playing there by herself.

5
00:00:32.248 --> 00:00:33.970
No one would even know she was there.

6
00:00:35.451 --> 00:00:40.074
She put the key in her pocket, and strolled up and down her favorite path outside the walled

7
00:00:40.114 --> 00:00:45.979
gardens. Nobody but Mary ever seemed to go there, so she could walk slowly and look at the

8
00:00:46.039 --> 00:00:46.860
wall closely.

9
00:00:47.941 --> 00:00:51.944
But no matter how carefully she looked, she could see nothing but ivy.

10
00:00:53.185 --> 00:00:54.526
She was very disappointed.

11
00:00:54.546 --> 00:01:01.732
"It seems so silly," she said, "to be near that garden and not be able to get in!"

12
00:01:02.888 --> 00:01:05.650
She decided that she would always carry the key with her.

13
00:01:06.831 --> 00:01:10.254
That way, if she ever found the hidden door, she would be ready.

14
00:01:11.435 --> 00:01:16.318
The next morning when Mary woke up, Martha was back at work and full of stories about her

15
00:01:16.358 --> 00:01:16.819
day off.

16
00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:22.803
She left the room for a moment and came back, holding something under her apron.

17
00:01:22.823 --> 00:01:25.785
"I've brought you a present!"

18
00:01:26.306 --> 00:01:27.346
she said with a grin.

19
00:01:27.366 --> 00:01:29.228
"A present!"

20
00:01:30.364 --> 00:01:36.007
Mary wondered how someone from a cottage filled with so many hungry people could give anyone a present.

21
00:01:36.027 --> 00:01:40.390
"A man was driving across the moor selling things.

22
00:01:41.611 --> 00:01:46.073
He stopped his cart at our door, and Mother bought you a skipping rope with some of my

23
00:01:46.093 --> 00:01:51.916
wages." Martha brought the rope out from underneath her apron and held it out proudly.

24
00:01:53.097 --> 00:01:54.118
Mary looked puzzled.

25
00:01:54.618 --> 00:01:55.518
"What's it for?"

26
00:01:55.538 --> 00:01:57.920
"Just watch me."

27
00:01:59.380 --> 00:02:05.183
Martha ran to the next room and, taking a handle in each hand, began to skip and skip.

28
00:02:06.524 --> 00:02:07.925
She counted up to a hundred.

29
00:02:07.945 --> 00:02:12.228
"I used to be able to skip to five hundred, but I'm out of practice."

30
00:02:13.468 --> 00:02:15.369
"Do you think I could ever skip like that?"

31
00:02:15.389 --> 00:02:20.412
"You just try," urged Martha, handing her the rope.

32
00:02:20.432 --> 00:02:26.356
"You can't skip to a hundred at first, but Mother says nothing will do you more good.

33
00:02:27.392 --> 00:02:29.853
You'll soon build up strength in your arms and legs."

34
00:02:30.934 --> 00:02:32.315
Mary tried skipping rope.

35
00:02:33.335 --> 00:02:37.317
She was not very good at it, but she liked it so much that she did not want

36
00:02:37.337 --> 00:02:37.858
to stop.

37
00:02:37.878 --> 00:02:42.780
"Put on your things and skip outdoors," said Martha.

38
00:02:43.961 --> 00:02:45.442
Mary put on her coat and hat.

39
00:02:46.002 --> 00:02:48.584
She opened the door to go out and then turned back.

40
00:02:49.884 --> 00:02:55.407
"Martha," she said slowly, "those were your wages that your mother used to buy this skipping rope.

41
00:02:55.847 --> 00:02:56.408
Thank you."

42
00:02:57.660 --> 00:02:58.821
She held out her hand.

43
00:03:00.463 --> 00:03:02.084
Martha laughed and shook her hand.

44
00:03:02.104 --> 00:03:04.246
"Now run outside and play."

45
00:03:05.707 --> 00:03:09.471
Mary skipped around the fountain and up one path and down another.

46
00:03:10.652 --> 00:03:16.116
She skipped into the kitchen garden and saw Ben Weatherstaff digging and talking to his robin.

47
00:03:16.136 --> 00:03:19.499
"Well!" he exclaimed.

48
00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:21.942
"You've skipped the roses into your cheeks.

49
00:03:22.982 --> 00:03:24.484
I wouldn't have believed that you could do it."

50
00:03:25.428 --> 00:03:26.529
"I never skipped before.

51
00:03:26.549 --> 00:03:28.250
I can only go up to twenty."

52
00:03:28.270 --> 00:03:30.011
"Keep going.

53
00:03:30.291 --> 00:03:31.132
You're doing fine."

54
00:03:31.632 --> 00:03:32.853
Ben nodded in approval.

55
00:03:32.873 --> 00:03:35.655
"Just see how the robin's watching you.

56
00:03:36.095 --> 00:03:37.516
He followed after you yesterday.

57
00:03:37.936 --> 00:03:39.197
He'll be at it again today."

58
00:03:40.758 --> 00:03:45.281
Mary skipped around all the gardens and the orchard, resting every few minutes.

59
00:03:46.602 --> 00:03:50.725
At last she made up her mind to try to skip the length of her favorite path.

60
00:03:51.966 --> 00:03:54.848
She counted up to thirty before she had to stop and rest.

61
00:03:56.124 --> 00:04:00.005
Suddenly she spotted the robin swaying on a long branch of ivy.

62
00:04:00.025 --> 00:04:04.866
"You showed me where the key was yesterday," she said.

63
00:04:04.886 --> 00:04:09.548
"You ought to show me the door today, but I don't believe you know where it is."

64
00:04:10.708 --> 00:04:13.529
The robin flew from the ivy to the top of the wall.

65
00:04:14.629 --> 00:04:17.990
He opened his beak and sang a loud, lovely trill.

66
00:04:18.610 --> 00:04:20.030
Mary laughed with delight.

67
00:04:21.111 --> 00:04:25.472
She stepped closer to him, and what happened next seemed like magic.

68
00:04:27.088 --> 00:04:30.491
A gust of wind swung aside some loose ivy trails.

69
00:04:31.752 --> 00:04:34.595
Mary jumped forward and caught the ivy in her hand.

70
00:04:35.856 --> 00:04:39.659
She'd seen something under the ivy—a round doorknob.

71
00:04:40.820 --> 00:04:42.642
She began to push the leaves aside.

72
00:04:43.783 --> 00:04:48.287
The ivy was thick, but nearly all of it hung in a loose, swinging curtain.

73
00:04:49.488 --> 00:04:53.792
Mary's heart thumped, and her hands shook with excitement as she touched the door.

74
00:04:55.452 --> 00:04:57.475
Mary reached into her pocket for the key.

75
00:04:58.637 --> 00:05:00.619
She put the key in the lock and turned it.

76
00:05:01.781 --> 00:05:06.247
And then she took a breath and looked up the long path to see if anyone was coming.

77
00:05:07.809 --> 00:05:08.730
No one was there.

78
00:05:09.311 --> 00:05:12.796
She took another breath and slowly opened the door.
